Monitor and GPU settings

Open Settings from Dome's universal menu or press Super+comma. Choose Graphical
Settings for GPU selection, connected monitor controls, and XR geometry. Keybindings have their own submenu, alongside Appearance and Keyboard
Layouts. Escape or Back returns to Settings and keeps the parent selection.

Each connected monitor offers enable/disable, supported resolution and refresh,
X/Y position, automatic arrangement, and primary-display selection. Changes are
saved in the Dome profile and applied to the current native session. Disabled
monitors remain listed so they can be enabled again; their saved mode and
position remain visible. The final physical display cannot be disabled here.

Compositor GPU selection is saved for the next native Dome session. Automatic
keeps the session default. Available PCI GPUs have model names and DRM card
identifiers; preferences use stable PCI aliases when available, so card-number
changes do not change the chosen GPU. A removed GPU falls back to the session
default. This controls DomeD's compositor GPU; the OpenXR runtime assigns its own
rendering/encoding device. An explicit DOME_DRM_DEVICE launch override takes
precedence, followed by the saved selection, a compiled device default, and the
seat's normal device selection. No session is restarted by saving a selection.

Up/Down wrap from the first setting to the last and back. Page Up/Page Down and
scroll navigation wrap as well. Home/End go to the beginning/end. The selected
row stays visible, and painting uses the same row spacing as pointer hit tests.
Left/Right cycle a setting's choices, including GPU Automatic and supported
monitor modes. Keybinding Help opens directly at the binding section.

Validation uses GPU discovery/configuration unit tests, disabled-monitor rows,
wrap/scroll tests, and a private FLTK/XR shell integration test for pointer
alignment, GPU preference persistence, and wrapped keyboard navigation.

The compositor and dome-xr renderer must be installed from the same build: the
shared scene/configuration schema is strict. Install both release binaries
together. Scene decoding errors are reported by dome-xr instead of silently
running an empty XR session.
